### Definition of ArrayDeque & Circular Array Architecture

A **`java.util.ArrayDeque<E>`** is a resizable, double-ended array queue that implements the `Deque<E>` interface. It is the gold-standard recommendation in modern Java for both FIFO queues and LIFO stacks.

Internally, `ArrayDeque` uses a circular array buffer:
```java
// OpenJDK ArrayDeque Storage Fields
transient Object[] elements;
transient int head;
transient int tail;
```
Elements are inserted and removed using two integer indices:
* `head`: Index of the current first element.
* `tail`: Index at which the next element will be added via `addLast()`.

---

### Blazing-Fast Bitmask Pointer Arithmetic

Because capacity is guaranteed to always be a power of two ($2^k$), the bitmask `(elements.length - 1)` contains all 1s in binary (e.g., $16 - 1 = 15 = \text{00001111}_2$).

When advancing the tail pointer:
$$\text{tail} = (\text{tail} + 1) \& (\text{elements.length} - 1)$$
When moving the head pointer backward:
$$\text{head} = (\text{head} - 1) \& (\text{elements.length} - 1)$$
If `tail` is $15$ in a 16-element array, $(15 + 1) \& 15 = 16 \& 15 = 0$. The pointer wraps back to index $0$ in a single CPU cycle without branching or division!

---

### ArrayDeque vs LinkedList: Why ArrayDeque Wins

| Metric | `ArrayDeque<E>` | `LinkedList<E>` |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Backing Storage** | Contiguous `Object[]` array | Dispersed `Node` objects on Heap |
| **Allocation Cost** | 0 allocations on add/poll | 1 new `Node` object allocated per `add()` |
| **Memory per Element** | 4–8 bytes (array slot reference) | 24–32 bytes (`item`, `prev`, `next`, header) |
| **CPU Cache Locality** | Optimal (sequential L1/L2 prefetching) | Poor (pointer chasing across heap) |
| **Null Elements** | Prohibited (throws `NullPointerException`) | Permitted |

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class ArrayDequeInternalsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Deque<String> circularQueue = new ArrayDeque<>(4);

        // Enqueue 3 items
        circularQueue.offer("Task_1");
        circularQueue.offer("Task_2");
        circularQueue.offer("Task_3");
        System.out.println("Queue content: " + circularQueue);

        // Poll 1 item (head advances)
        System.out.println("Polled head: " + circularQueue.poll());

        // Add 2 more items (tail wraps around circular buffer!)
        circularQueue.offer("Task_4");
        circularQueue.offer("Task_5");

        System.out.println("After wrap-around: " + circularQueue);
        System.out.println("Next to process:  " + circularQueue.peek());
    }
}
```

**Console Output:**
```text
Queue content: [Task_1, Task_2, Task_3]
Polled head: Task_1
After wrap-around: [Task_2, Task_3, Task_4, Task_5]
Next to process:  Task_2
```

